1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,400
Hi! We're Group 14, Team Sweet Potatoes.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:08,308
We studied how international residents in
Melbourne take part in council decisions.

3
00:00:09,008 --> 00:00:11,465
Our investigation found five problems.

4
00:00:11,465 --> 00:00:15,978
One: even when it's translated,
council language is hard to understand.

5
00:00:15,978 --> 00:00:19,483
Two: time, distance and fees
keep people from attending.

6
00:00:19,483 --> 00:00:23,607
Three: people only take part when
an issue feels personally relevant.

7
00:00:23,607 --> 00:00:27,617
Four: they don't know which department
to contact, or whether they were heard.

8
00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:30,617
Five: council news arrives
by mail and flyers,

9
00:00:30,617 --> 00:00:33,095
but newer residents live on Instagram.

10
00:00:33,995 --> 00:00:35,330
Let's meet Jason.

11
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:38,225
He's 34, has lived in
Melbourne for eight years,

12
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:40,676
and English isn't his first language.

13
00:00:40,676 --> 00:00:45,184
One Saturday morning, a new park rule
limits when his dog can be off the leash.

14
00:00:45,184 --> 00:00:48,716
He disagrees, but doesn't know who
to tell, or which words to use.

15
00:00:49,416 --> 00:00:51,018
So we introduce HearHere:

16
00:00:51,018 --> 00:00:56,273
a digital device that combines a physical
information pillar with an AI customised

17
00:00:56,273 --> 00:00:56,942
helper.

18
00:00:56,942 --> 00:01:00,496
Hi there! I'm Spud, the Street
Post for Understanding Decisions.

19
00:01:00,496 --> 00:01:02,098
Here's the post from every side.

20
00:01:04,698 --> 00:01:09,081
It connects three channels. Spud's
official account shares only verified

21
00:01:09,081 --> 00:01:09,933
council posts,

22
00:01:09,933 --> 00:01:12,040
where residents already scroll.

23
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:16,353
An AI agent answers questions, and
the HearHere app tracks each issue.

24
00:01:17,153 --> 00:01:22,421
Back to Saturday, 8 a.m. Jason
walks his dog past a HearHere post.

25
00:01:22,421 --> 00:01:26,184
Its pillar shows verified
council and community notices.

26
00:01:26,184 --> 00:01:28,112
Hi! Got something to say? I'm all ears.

27
00:01:28,112 --> 00:01:31,404
The screen under Spud lists
nearby issues by distance,

28
00:01:31,404 --> 00:01:34,087
including a new rule
for the park next door.

29
00:01:34,787 --> 00:01:36,574
Jason taps the park issue.

30
00:01:36,574 --> 00:01:39,606
A plain-language summary
appears in his own language,

31
00:01:39,606 --> 00:01:41,599
with difficult terms explained.

32
00:01:41,599 --> 00:01:44,655
Now he simply talks to
Spud, in his own language.

33
00:01:44,655 --> 00:01:48,351
The new park rule makes walking my dog
really inconvenient. I hope it can be

34
00:01:48,351 --> 00:01:49,203
reconsidered.

35
00:01:49,203 --> 00:01:53,904
Spud turns it into a clear English
comment, names the responsible department,

36
00:01:53,904 --> 00:01:56,261
and lets Jason edit before sending.

37
00:01:56,261 --> 00:01:57,597
No audio is stored.

38
00:01:58,497 --> 00:02:01,107
Jason taps his phone on the NFC pad.

39
00:02:01,107 --> 00:02:05,473
The issue and his comment move to the
HearHere app, and the post keeps nothing.

40
00:02:05,473 --> 00:02:08,246
Tracking begins only
because Jason chose it.

41
00:02:08,246 --> 00:02:10,989
The app shows each stage:
Received, In review,

42
00:02:10,989 --> 00:02:14,727
then an outcome with a case
number, or reasons if declined.

43
00:02:14,727 --> 00:02:18,246
Each new HearHere post Jason
visits also earns a badge:

44
00:02:18,246 --> 00:02:20,230
a small reward for taking part.

45
00:02:21,130 --> 00:02:24,434
Jason can also share the
issue as a card on Instagram,

46
00:02:24,434 --> 00:02:26,269
so his neighbours can join in.

47
00:02:27,269 --> 00:02:31,564
For Jason, that means relevant issues
on his route, in his own language,

48
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:34,229
a clear department, and visible progress.

49
00:02:34,229 --> 00:02:35,696
All without attending a meeting.

50
00:02:36,396 --> 00:02:37,799
HearHere has limits.

51
00:02:37,799 --> 00:02:41,514
Speaking in public may feel
exposing, AI can mistranslate,

52
00:02:41,514 --> 00:02:44,744
and our fieldwork didn't
include council staff.

53
00:02:44,744 --> 00:02:49,396
Next, we'll test a low-fidelity prototype
with residents across languages.

54
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:53,929
HearHere, with Spud: the Street
Post for Understanding Decisions.

55
00:02:53,929 --> 00:02:55,021
Speak here. Be heard!
